# converted from 06-sallows-rest.md
@title Sallow's Rest
@bg hollow
@mood combat

narrate He did not send them.
narrate He wanted that understood, and there was nobody left to understand it, so he understood it himself, in the way you keep a light on in a room nobody visits.
narrate The faceless things were not soldiers. They were not even his. They were what was left of people who had stood at the hole in the field and asked for something and paid everything — every memory, every name, every reason they had walked there in the first place. What was left did not die. It simply had nowhere to be.
narrate They wandered. They drifted. They went where the Mire was thin.
narrate And on a wet evening in the eleventh year of his exile, forty-one of them drifted into a hamlet called Sallow's Rest because there was a smell of people there, and they did what a thing with no memory does when it finds a thing with memory: they took it.
narrate Cael learned about it afterwards. He learned about it the way he learned about everything: late, and by counting.
narrate He was three miles south when he felt it.
narrate That was the part he never told anyone, because it sounded like a lie and because the truth of it was worse. He felt it — a thinning, a wrongness, a sense of something in the Mire being spent that should not have been spent. He had carried the mark on his palm for eleven years and it had made him sensitive to a particular kind of quiet, and the quiet at Sallow's Rest that evening was the loudest thing he had ever felt.
narrate He walked three miles in forty minutes.
narrate He arrived too late, which is the only way he ever arrived anywhere.
narrate He stood in the trees at the edge of the square and watched.
narrate He could see all of it. The militia, mostly dead. The faceless things, unhurried. A man on the ground with a spear, holding them off the way you hold off weather — and Cael's whole body went cold, because he knew that stance, he had watched that stance come up to a rope between two posts eleven years ago with a ledger in its hand.
narrate Eleven years. And here he was, at the end of the world, still holding a line.
narrate Cael stood in the trees and did not move.
narrate And then a boy got up.
narrate Cael saw it happen. He saw the whole of it, in the way you see a thing you will think about for the rest of your life: a young soldier on the ground, bleeding out of a shoulder, ten feet from a man who was dying — and then, slowly, badly, with a body that had stopped answering, getting up.
narrate He did not get up well. He did not get up heroically. He got up like a man climbing a hill, one piece at a time, and he fell once and got up again, and he got to the other man, and the other man — Cael could see his mouth move — told him to go back.
narrate And the boy did not go back.
narrate Cael stood in the trees with his hand on a trunk and watched a nineteen-year-old refuse to leave someone, and something happened in his chest that he had not felt in eleven years and did not have a name for.
narrate He would work out the name later, in the black house, with a cushion and a ledger. It was envy.
narrate He could have helped.
narrate He wanted that understood too, and there was even less chance of that. He could have walked into that square. He had a mark on his palm and eleven years of practice and the faceless things were, in the end, his — not soldiers, but his — and he could have sent them away with a word.
narrate He stood in the trees and did the arithmetic.
narrate That was the arithmetic. He had done it four hundred times in eleven years and he was very good at it.
narrate He went north.
narrate He saved four people.
narrate And a boy in a square behind him got up anyway, and did not need him, and Cael walked eleven miles with that in his chest and did not once look back.
narrate He came back three days later.
narrate The graves were in the shrine yard, forty-two of them, and there was a man sitting on the wall by the well who was not a soldier and had not been a soldier for eleven years and was, in every way that mattered, the reason all of this had happened.
narrate Cael stood in the trees and looked at Bryn for a long time.
narrate He could have walked out. He had rehearsed it four hundred times: the things he would say, the way he would stand, the dignity of it. He had been ready to be angry for eleven years.
narrate And what he felt, standing in the trees watching an old man sit on a wall beside forty-two graves, was the exact thing he had felt in the reeds outside his own burning house.
narrate Because Bryn had heard it. Because Bryn had put his hand over his mouth in a garden eleven years ago and had spent eleven years being unable to say it out loud, and there was, in the end, a person in the world who was carrying the same thing he was carrying.
narrate Cael turned around and went back into the Mire.
narrate He did not speak to Bryn for two more days.
narrate And when he did — when he finally walked into a camp at dusk and sat down opposite a man who had aged eleven years in eleven years, which is not as much as it sounds — the first thing he said was:
corin neutral There's a boy coming.
